WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.899
The model asks, your code acts.

2
00:00:02.179 --> 00:00:06.829
Everything you have built so far ends with text: the model writes, your code reads.

3
00:00:07.109 --> 00:00:11.845
Tool calling (OpenAI and Google call it function calling) adds one move.

4
00:00:12.125 --> 00:00:21.811
You describe some functions to the model, and instead of answering in prose it can reply with a structured request: “call lookup_teammate with name equal to Priya.

5
00:00:22.091 --> 00:00:28.128
” Your code runs the function and sends the result back, and the model continues with the new information.

6
00:00:28.408 --> 00:00:30.157
The important word is request.

7
00:00:30.437 --> 00:00:33.253
For tools you define, the model executes nothing.

8
00:00:33.533 --> 00:00:36.968
It cannot reach your directory, your calendar, or your database.

9
00:00:37.248 --> 00:00:44.053
It produces a tool name and a JSON object of arguments, and your application decides whether that call happens.

10
00:00:44.333 --> 00:00:50.136
Anthropic’s docs put the split simply: Claude returns a structured call that your application runs.

11
00:00:50.416 --> 00:00:58.395
Microsoft’s Generative AI for Beginners course frames the benefit in two parts, and both apply to the Studio 1 project.

12
00:00:58.675 --> 00:01:05.117
First, consistent shape: the arguments arrive as structured data instead of free text you have to parse.

13
00:01:05.397 --> 00:01:14.869
Second, outside data: the model can use facts it never saw in training, like who is on your team or what date “next Friday” is relative to a meeting.

14
00:01:15.149 --> 00:01:19.437
In the meeting-notes tool you will build, a transcript says “Marcus will take it.

15
00:01:19.717 --> 00:01:22.875
” Only a lookup tells you which of your two Marcuses that is.
